research/audit/wave5-real-analysis.provenance.jsonl:{"id":"fs-consecutive-differences-null-implies-cauchy","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cauchy_sequence","https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Sequence_of_Square_Roots_of_Natural_Numbers_is_not_Cauchy","https://www.jirka.org/ra/"],"rationale":"Wikipedia Cauchy sequence states the exact caution this item refutes - that it is not sufficient for each term to become arbitrarily close to the PRECEDING term - and gives the very witness used here, the sequence sqrt(n), whose consecutive differences tend to 0 while the sequence is unbounded and hence not Cauchy. ProofWiki, 'Sequence of Square Roots of Natural Numbers is not Cauchy', proves that half. The item's construction is the false-statement framing of that sourced caution together with the identity sqrt(k+1) - sqrt(k) = 1/(sqrt(k+1) + sqrt(k)), so it is a reformulation of sourced material: ai-altered. The refutation is written locally from the library's square-root and Archimedean items, with the bound 1/(sqrt(k+1)+sqrt(k)) <= 1/sqrt(k+1) and a squaring comparison, so it is adapted rather than followed: ai-altered.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-05"}
